accession number   removed_accession_id  report status  updated type  surgical pathology pathology report  ms 08  removed_accession_id  case  ms 08  removed_accession_id  removed_patient_name  date taken  removed_date source care unit  same day surgery  wacc 3 path subspecialty service  breast 1 results to  michele annette gadd  m d  resident  anna mcdonald  m d  signed out by  ronald balassanian  m d             revised report            reason for amendment  1  corrected final diagnosis reason for addendum  1  additional immunohistochemistry results reason for amendment  2  corrected transcription error not in final diagnosis clinical data  the patient is a 60 year old woman with left breast enhancement seen on mri  a prior core biopsy demonstrated probable lobular neoplasia  final diagnosis  breast  left   excision  1  ductal carcinoma in situ  2  extensive lobular neoplasia  lobular carcinoma in situ  with calcifications   see table 1 and note   table of pathological findings  1 ductal carcinoma in situ  dcis  tumor size  involving 4 of 6 blocks  grade  3  calcifications  limited to the carcinoma in situ  both lcis and dcis   margin of ductal carcinoma in situ  positive along the inked margin of the specimen along a broad front  stains for receptors  requested on block  removed_accession_id   note  sections of the excision sample reveal large ducts distended by tumor cells  in many areas the tumor cells are large and pleomorphic with abundant cytoplasm and occasional single mucin vacuoles  a few cells have a signet ring appearance  the nuclei are mildly enlarged with mildly irregular contours and granular chromatin  the morphologic findings in these areas are compatible with pleomorphic variant of lobular carcinoma in situ  intermixed with these areas are other ducts with cells that have a similar appearance but with a distinctly cribriform pattern compatible with a component of dcis  an immunohistochemical stain for e cadherin on slide  removed_accession_id  is largely negative and supports the diagnosis of lcis  however  there is weak membrane staining in the ductal cells in the areas of cribriform architecture supporting a diagnosis dcis  stains for p63  myosin  calponin and keratin performed on slides  removed_accession_id    removed_accession_id   and  removed_accession_id  do not show evidence of an invasive carcinoma  overall  the morphologic findings are compatible with carcinoma in situ with both ductal and lobular features  immunostains for estrogen receptor and progesterone receptor will be reported in an addendum  this case was amended to correct the final diagnosis  on initial review  the findings were interpreted as extensive lcis  however  upon review of the e cadherin as well as the er  pr and her 2neu stain the findings were interpreted as both ductal and lobular carcinoma in situ  the final diagnosis and note in the report has been revised to reflect these changes  by his her signature below  the pathologist listed as making the final diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses  gross description  received fresh   labeled remove_patient_name  donovan  remove_mrn and  left breast needle loc   is an unoriented portion of fibroadipose tissue  5 0 x removed_date x 0 6   with two needle localization wires in place  the specimen is entirely inked black and serially sectioned to reveal no mass lesions  the specimen is entirely included in  removed_accession_id  with   prior biopsy site in  removed_accession_id   addendum note  reason  1 quantitative results of immunohistochemical staining for receptor proteins breast  left  block  removed_accession_id    the ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells do not stain for estrogen receptor protein  the ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein  the ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells show strong  3   overexpression of her 2 c erbb 2  see note  note  estrogen and proges